      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2507.00711v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span>&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2507.00711v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2507.00711v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2507.00711v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Large <span class="search-hit mathjax">Language</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Models</span> (LLMs) trained via <span class="search-hit mathjax">Reinforcement</span> <span class="search-hit mathjax">Learning</span> (RL) have recently achieved impressive results on reasoning benchmarks. Yet, growing evidence shows that these <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> often generate longer but ineffective chains of thought (CoTs), calling into question whether benchmark gains reflect real reasoning improvements. We present new evidence of overthinking, where <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> disregard correct solutions even when explicitly provided, instead continuing to generate unnecessary reasoning steps that often lead to incorrect conclusions. Experiments on three state-of-the-art <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> using the AIME2024 math benchmark reveal critical limitations in these <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> ability to integrate corrective information, posing new challenges for achieving robust and interpretable reasoning.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2507.00711v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2507.00711v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

